# Plans

Monthly premiums are for the employee.

table = Plan,Monthly premium,Annual maximum,Deductible,Orthodontia; Basic,$14,"$1,000",$75,None; Plus,$28,"$1,500",$50,None; Premier,$41,"$2,500",$25,"50% up to a $1,500 lifetime maximum"; Family Max,$63,"$3,000",None,"50% up to a $2,000 lifetime maximum"

# Annual premium

The annual employee premium is 12 times the monthly premium.

data prem = plan,annual; Basic,168; Plus,336; Premier,492; Family Max,756

chart bar prem y=annual unit=$ labels title="Annual employee premium"

Family Max costs $264 more a year than Premier.

# Who each plan suits

In the pulse survey, 38 employees said they have children who will need braces soon. That is why orthodontia matters.

- **Basic:** the lowest premium, with no orthodontia cover.
- **Plus:** a middle option, with a $1,500 annual maximum, a $50 deductible and no orthodontia.
- **Premier:** orthodontia at 50 percent, a $25 deductible and a $2,500 annual maximum.
- **Family Max:** no deductible, and orthodontia up to $2,000 for life. It suits families with ongoing orthodontia.

# Recommendation

We recommend Premier as the default plan. Family Max costs $264 more a year and only pays off for families with ongoing orthodontia.
